<h2>Graph Databases &amp; GraphRAG — Store the Relationships, Change the Answers</h2>

<p class="scn-sub">An introductory explainer that connects the dots in one pass: from graph databases that store relationships instead of tables, to knowledge graphs built on top of them, to GraphRAG — using that graph to lift the quality of AI answers. Concepts and examples first.</p>

<h3>Three unfamiliar words, up front</h3>

<ul>
<li><strong>Graph database (graph DB)</strong> — a database that stores data as <strong>dots (nodes) and lines (edges)</strong> rather than tables. The relationship itself — &ldquo;who did what&rdquo; — is the basic unit of storage.</li>
<li><strong>RAG</strong> — Retrieval-Augmented Generation: every time you ask an AI a question, the system <strong>fetches relevant snippets from a corpus on the spot</strong> and staples them to the prompt. Like an open-book exam, the model lays out its evidence before answering.</li>
<li><strong>GraphRAG</strong> — a RAG whose corpus is stored not as loose snippets but as a <strong>connected graph</strong>, so retrieval can pull in the surrounding context along with each hit.</li>
</ul>

<p>These three form a single chain: <strong>store relationships (graph DB) &rarr; knowledge becomes a web (knowledge graph) &rarr; search the web whole and the answer gains evidence (GraphRAG).</strong></p>

<h3>Graph DB — pathfinding instead of JOINs</h3>

<p>The database most of us know is the relational database (RDB). Data piles up <strong>table by table</strong> — a members table, a ratings table, a movies table — and relationships between tables are reconstructed at query time with <strong>JOINs</strong> (an operation that stitches two tables together on a shared column). Two tables need one join; five tables need four — the deeper the relationship, the higher the stitching cost.</p>

<p>A graph DB goes the other way: <strong>relationships are stored as arrows (edges) from the start</strong>. When the question &ldquo;which action movies did person A rate highly?&rdquo; arrives, you don't stitch tables — you start at node A, walk the rating edge, then walk one more genre edge, and you've arrived. It transplants the way people trace relationships in their heads almost directly into a data structure, so a drawn graph reads like a sentence — <em>Person A &rarr;(RATED 5.0)&rarr; Movie X &rarr;(GENRE)&rarr; Action</em>.</p>

