<p>The formal name for this storage model is the <strong>property graph</strong>. Both nodes and edges can carry <strong>labels</strong> (kinds: person, movie&hellip;) and <strong>properties</strong> (name, score, date&hellip;), so it holds real-world subject&ndash;verb&ndash;object almost verbatim. Neo4j is the best-known implementation, and this wiki's own <a href="#!kgs-edges">knowledge-graph model</a> belongs to the same property-graph family.</p>

<h3>Cypher — how you talk to a graph</h3>

<p>As RDBs have SQL, graph DBs have a query language called <strong>Cypher</strong>. The fun part: its syntax <strong>looks like the drawing</strong> — nodes go in parentheses <code>( )</code>, edges in brackets <code>[ ]</code>, direction as arrows. The question above, in Cypher:</p>

<pre><code><span class="c">// Action movies A rated 4.0 or higher — the pattern is the picture</span>
MATCH (a:Person {name: 'A'})-[r:RATED]->(m:Movie)-[:GENRE]->(:Genre {name: 'Action'})
WHERE r.score >= 4.0
RETURN m.title, r.score</code></pre>

<p>Read <code>(a:Person)-[r:RATED]-&gt;(m:Movie)</code> aloud and it says &ldquo;person a rated movie m.&rdquo; No assembling joins in your head — the feel of graph querying is <strong>drawing the shape of the relationship you want and asking for it</strong>.</p>

<h3>RAG refresher — the open-book exam</h3>

<p>RAG is the standard technique for pinning evidence to an AI's answers. When a question arrives, the system ①searches the corpus for relevant snippets (Retrieval), ②staples them onto the question (Augmented), and ③has the model generate the answer from inside them (Generation). Instead of confabulating from memory (hallucination), the model gives <strong>an answer with sources</strong>. What sets it apart from web search is <strong>your own data</strong> — internal documents, personal notes, equipment manuals that the web has never seen. It is like giving an organization's expert a drawer from which their tacit knowledge (experience never written down) can be pulled at any time.</p>

<p>Ordinary RAG puts the corpus in a <strong>vector database</strong>. Documents are long, so they are cut into fixed-size <strong>chunks</strong>, each converted to a list of numbers carrying its meaning (an embedding), and the chunks semantically closest to the question are fetched. So far so good. The problem: <strong>the cutting severs context</strong> — chunks become strangers to one another, and retrieval can no longer see which document or chapter a chunk belongs to, or which chunks it connects with.</p>

<h3>GraphRAG — stitch the chunks, fetch the neighborhood</h3>

<p>GraphRAG sews that gap up with a graph. Chunks become <strong>nodes</strong>, and the relationships between them — same document, adjacent order, mention of the same entity (person, product, event) — become <strong>edges</strong>. Retrieval turns into two steps: first a vector search finds chunks close to the question (cast a wide net), then the retriever follows edges to pull in the neighboring context (walk deep). Not one snippet but the snippet's whole <strong>neighborhood</strong> becomes the evidence.</p>

