<p>The payoff shows up in three ways.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Multi-hop reasoning</strong> — &ldquo;the latest news in the politics category&rdquo; is an awkward question for pure vector search (you're not looking for snippets that <em>resemble</em> the word &lsquo;politics&rsquo;). With a graph you walk <em>category node &rarr; its articles &rarr; each article's content chunks</em> and collect exactly that set. The collected articles are themselves the answer's list of evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Hierarchy and membership stay visible</strong> — which document and chapter a chunk belongs to, which documents relate to it, all survive as structure, so the model answers <strong>knowing the shape</strong> rather than staring at one orphaned chunk.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence you can draw</strong> — you can show, as a graph picture, which nodes the answer stepped on. The answer ships with its own map, which makes verifying and trusting it far easier.</li>
</ul>

<p>In short, the model stays the same; what changes is <strong>the weave of the context you hand it</strong>. If the answer sits quietly inside one or two passages, vector RAG is enough; GraphRAG earns its keep when the answer spans <strong>relationships scattered across documents</strong>. In practice a hybrid — cast with vectors, walk with the graph — is the common build.</p>

<h3>Ontology and graph schema — the blueprint of the web</h3>

<p>Before piling up a graph, something must be decided: <strong>which kinds of nodes and which kinds of relationships you will admit</strong>. That rule book is the <strong>ontology</strong> (a standard frame for organizing knowledge), and its concrete form in a graph DB's labels and properties is the <strong>graph schema</strong>. It is the work of writing down, as a specification, the concepts people use tacitly at work — so that the AI reads the same map humans do.</p>

<p>Education makes the intuition easy. A knowledge map that records <strong>prerequisite relations</strong> between concepts — &ldquo;equations come before quadratic functions&rdquo; — lets a tutoring system do better than drilling a struggling student on quadratics again and again: it can recommend the prerequisite equation problems one step upstream. The ontology becomes the <strong>baseline for inference</strong>. The taxonomy work product teams do for analytics — naming and nesting pages, sub-pages, click events — is the same lineage of work; an ontology is the larger frame that also records what each relationship <em>means</em>.</p>

<p>Put in order: <strong>ontology (which relationships are admitted) &rarr; graph schema (how they are written as labels/properties) &rarr; knowledge graph (the web filled with real data) &rarr; GraphRAG (searching that web)</strong>. Most of GraphRAG's quality is decided at this design stage.</p>

<h3>A similarly named stranger — LangGraph</h3>

<p>Because of the &lsquo;graph&rsquo; in its name, <strong>LangGraph</strong> is often confused with all of the above. It is not a graph that stores knowledge but <strong>a framework for wiring an AI agent's workflow</strong> — each <strong>work step becomes a node</strong> (analyze intent, retrieve, generate), and edges define in what order and under what conditions steps run. So: GraphRAG's graph is a <strong>map of knowledge</strong>; LangGraph's graph is a <strong>flowchart of work</strong>. They are commonly used together — the &lsquo;retrieve&rsquo; node of a LangGraph agent calling GraphRAG inside. Whether the flow runs as intended can be watched step by step in LangGraph Studio, its visual debugger.</p>

<h3>Where it gets used</h3>

<p><strong>Manufacturing and smart factories</strong> are a good example. Write the flood of failure logs as a graph of <em>equipment &rarr; part &rarr; symptom &rarr; cause &rarr; fix</em>, and when a new failure appears you reach past cases with the same symptom — and their verified fixes — by simply following the edges. A news service becomes <em>category &rarr; article &rarr; content chunk</em>; education becomes <em>concept &rarr; prerequisite &rarr; exercise</em> — the web's shape differs by domain, but the same method works anywhere the answer leans on how things relate to each other. This is also where hyper-personalization connects. The links you know tacitly have to be written down as a graph before an AI can walk them the way you do.</p>

<h3>Getting started — draw it, export it, extract it automatically</h3>

<p><strong>Step one is simply drawing.</strong> <a href="https://arrows.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener">arrows.app</a>, a free web tool from Neo4j Labs, is a whiteboard where you sketch graphs by dragging nodes around, mind-map style. Take whatever is in your head — or a workflow you want to automate — and spill it out as nodes and arrows: that is, in effect, drafting your ontology. A finished drawing exports to PNG and SVG, and — the useful part — <strong>to Cypher statements</strong>, which you can pour straight into a graph DB and turn into a real knowledge graph.</p>

<p><strong>Step two is turning documents into graphs automatically.</strong> Neo4j's official Python package <code>neo4j-graphrag</code> ships a knowledge-graph construction pipeline (<code>SimpleKGPipeline</code>): feed it a PDF and it runs <strong>chunk splitting &rarr; entity &amp; relation extraction &rarr; embedding &rarr; graph loading</strong> in one pass.</p>

<pre><code><span class="c"># One PDF to a knowledge graph — this is essentially the whole line</span>
pipeline = SimpleKGPipeline(
    llm=llm, driver=neo4j_driver,
    embedder=embedder, from_pdf=True,
)
await pipeline.run_async(file_path="document.pdf")</code></pre>

<p>Afterwards the document's entities and relations sit extracted in the graph, and the same package's retrievers carry you on to GraphRAG queries that blend vector search with graph traversal. When &ldquo;how do I even turn a document into a graph?&rdquo; feels daunting, this is the fastest first experiment. One more thing worth keeping: a RAG isn't done when it's built — it becomes usable when you evaluate whether it gives the answers you wanted.</p>

<h3>Where this wiki fits</h3>

<p>This wiki does not run GraphRAG. But its raw material is the same lineage — a <a href="#!kgs-overview">knowledge system</a> that extracts and links concepts per document, the <a href="#!kgs-edges">property-graph model</a> standing on top, and a machine-consumable <code>knowledge-graph.json</code> export. In this article's vocabulary, this wiki is <strong>a GraphRAG-ready corpus that already owns its ontology (the kgs document set) and its knowledge graph (the index)</strong>. If you want to touch a graph with your hands, placing nodes and edges in this wiki's <a href="#!graph">Graph Studio</a> is practice in the same sense as arrows.app.</p>

